  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/psy#moralStages">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#DevelopmentalPsychologyTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">stages of moral development</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Kohlberg, *Essays on Moral Development*, vol. 1 (Harper &amp; Row, 1981); Gilligan (1982)</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">pre-conventional, conventional, post-conventional</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">stages of moral development</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Developmental levels of moral reasoning</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">The Heinz dilemma</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Built on Piaget (1932); 1958 dissertation with an all-male sample; revised by neo-Kohlbergians (Rest, 1999)</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">stages of moral development</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Reasoning about justice; criticised for ranking care lower</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Ethics Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Ethics review, `developmentalAltitude` row</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Developmental psychology</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Developmental altitude is the most powerful and most dangerous attribute in the structure. Powerful because it captures something real: lenses differ in how widely and reflectively they extend concern, and a lens organised around self-interest is not equivalent to one organised around universal principles. Dangerous because every scale is built from somewhere. Kohlberg's stages, developed from interviews with boys and men, placed rule- and rights-based reasoning at the top and relational reasoning lower, so women's responses scored lower. Gilligan's critique is a documented case of an altitude scale encoding its authors' perspective, and it parallels the Confucian and Stoic rankings noted in the Classical review. For AI, altitude matters because developers increasingly claim their systems reason at a "higher" moral level, or align to "universal" values. Modelling implication: `developmentalAltitude` should never be stored as a bare value. It should always be an assessed property with `assessedBy` pointing to the scale and its PerspectiveType, so that the ontology records "post-conventional according to Kohlberg's scale" rather than "post-conventional". Rival altitude scales then become comparable, and their disagreements become Divergences.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:explanationProvenance xml:lang="en">verbatim, [[v-0.01 EthicsCouncil Review of - PerspectiveStructure 0.01|Ethics review]]</ho:explanationProvenance>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Jean Piaget; Lawrence Kohlberg; Carol Gilligan; James Rest</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">levels of moral reasoning, not of concern directly</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#developmentalAltitude"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">1981</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
