  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/lit#omelas">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#LiteratureTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Omelas</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Le Guin, "The Ones Who Walk Away from Omelas", *New Dimensions 3* (1973)</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Omelas</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">accepted harm</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">the child in the basement</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A harm the lens's holders know about and accept as the price of a benefit</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">Known error rates for some groups, accepted for overall accuracy</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Drew on William James (1891); Hugo Award 1974</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Omelas</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Fiction; the mechanism, not a real case</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Sci-Fi Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Sci-Fi review, `acceptedHarm` row</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Literature</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">In Le Guin's story, a city's happiness depends on one child kept in misery, and everyone knows. That isn't a blind spot; it is an accepted harm, seen and agreed to as the price of a benefit. As a spoke, it defines the accepted kind and shows why it must be kept apart from the others. A blind spot calls for better instruments. An accepted harm calls for moral and political argument, because nothing is hidden: the lens has made a trade-off and needs to defend it. Lumping the two together lets accepted harm shelter behind the excuse of ignorance. The story also names a response the other kinds lack: walking away, or exit. For AI, many harms are accepted, not missed: known error rates for some groups, known trauma to content moderators, known environmental costs of training. Recording them as accepted, linked to `costBearer` and `differentialBurden`, makes the trade-off explicit. The grade is closeMatch, with the caveat that it is fiction: it gives the mechanism, not evidence of any real case.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Ursula K. Le Guin; William James</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">a seen harm, knowingly accepted for a benefit</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#acceptedHarm"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">1973</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
