  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/psy#careStages">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#DevelopmentalPsychologyTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">selfish → care → universal care → integrated (Wilber's rendering)</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Gilligan, *In a Different Voice* (Harvard UP, 1982); stage labels as rendered in Wilber, "The Leading Edge of the Unknown in the Human Being"</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">"different voice"</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">ethics of care</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">selfish → care → universal care → integrated (Wilber's rendering)</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Stages of moral development described in the voice of care and responsibility, from care for oneself to care for all</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A caregiver who moves from protecting only her own family to caring about everyone's children</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Written as an answer to Kohlberg, whose scale scored care reasoning lower</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">selfish → care → universal care → integrated (Wilber's rendering)</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Built from the care voice; Wilber's labels may simplify Gilligan's own</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Claude Opus 5.5 (this session)</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Gilligan 1982: Ethics review, `normativeForm: relational` row. The four labels are Wilber's rendering, read in his primary text; Gilligan's own wording of her levels has not been checked.</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Developmental psychology (ethics of care)</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Gilligan's *In a Different Voice* answered Kohlberg's scale by describing moral development in the voice of care and responsibility, not only rights and justice. As Wilber renders her, the stages run from selfish (care for oneself), to care (care extended to one's own group), to universal care (care for all people), to integrated (both voices brought together). As a spoke, it matters for two reasons. First, it is the most direct match to the hub: its stages are defined by how widely care extends. Second, it is the scale Wilber says his own four bands come from, so the Integral bands and Gilligan's stages can be mapped one-to-one on Wilber's authority. That reverses how the first draft of this note treated Gilligan, as only a critic recorded under Divergences. The Kohlberg–Gilligan dispute stays a Divergence, but Gilligan's scale is now a spoke in its own right. One caution: the four labels here are Wilber's paraphrase. Gilligan's own description of her levels should be checked before any of her wording is quoted in the book.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:explanationProvenance xml:lang="en">new draft</ho:explanationProvenance>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Carol Gilligan; Nel Noddings; Ken Wilber (adopter)</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">stages of widening care, the most direct match to the hub's definition</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#developmentalAltitude"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">1982</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
