    <ho:displayGroup xml:lang="en">11 ASSESSMENTS (each an Assessment with assessedBy -&gt; PerspectiveType)</ho:displayGroup>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/cog#censors">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#CognitiveScienceTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">censors</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Minsky, *The Society of Mind* (1986), ch. 27</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">censors</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">suppressors</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Mechanisms within a mind that prevent certain thoughts or observations from forming</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A team culture in which some failure reports are never filed</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Minsky, ch. 27 "Censors and Jokes"</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">censors</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Individual mind; extended here to institutions by analogy</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Cognitive Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Cognitive review, `suppressors` row</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Cognitive science</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">In *The Society of Mind* (1986), Minsky described the mind as a society of smaller agents, including censors and suppressors that stop certain thoughts from forming at all. As a spoke, it defines the suppressed kind: the lens could see something, but a mechanism inside it prevents the observation from forming or being recorded. That distinguishes suppression from the other three. It isn't the price of focus (constitutive), a failure of care (negligent), or a seen and defended trade-off (accepted). The extension from minds to institutions is this ontology's analogy, not Minsky's claim, but it fits: organizations have internal censors too, in incentives, review processes and what gets logged. For AI harm, it explains why some blind spots don't yield to better instruments. If a team is discouraged from filing certain failure reports, a better detector changes nothing. Suppressed blind spots call for transparency, disclosure duties and whistleblower protection. Because suppression is easy to allege and hard to prove, it must always be an Assessment with evidence and `assessedBy`.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Marvin Minsky</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">internal agents that stop thoughts forming</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#suppressedBlindSpot"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">1986</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
