  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/law#willfulBlindness">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#LawTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">willful blindness</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">*Global-Tech Appliances, Inc. v. SEB S.A.*, 563 U.S. 754 (2011)</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">conscious avoidance</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">deliberate ignorance</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">willful blindness</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Believing a fact is highly probable and acting deliberately to avoid confirming it</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A platform that avoids checking whether known harmful content is still circulating (illustrative)</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Doctrine borrowed from criminal law; 8–1, Kennedy dissenting</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">willful blindness</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">A legal test, narrower than suppression in general</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Claude Opus 5.5 (this session)</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Web 2026-11-17, Cornell LII syllabus</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Law</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">In *Global-Tech Appliances v. SEB* (2011), the US Supreme Court set two requirements for willful blindness: the defendant must subjectively believe there is a high probability that a fact exists, and must take deliberate actions to avoid learning of it. The Court said this standard goes beyond recklessness and negligence. As a spoke, it is a narrow, tested case of the suppressed kind, with a useful feature: it separates suppression from negligence by requiring both belief and deliberate avoidance. That gives the ontology a concrete test for the hardest boundary in this hub. A lens that never suspected a harm may be negligent; a lens that suspected it and arranged not to find out is suppressing. The grade is narrowMatch because the doctrine is a legal standard for imputing knowledge, narrower than suppression in general. Justice Kennedy's dissent, that willful blindness is not knowledge, is worth recording too: it marks the line between what a lens knew and what it can be held to have known.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">(Supreme Court majority); Justice Kennedy (dissent)</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">narrowMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">one legal test for deliberate avoidance of knowledge</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#suppressedBlindSpot"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">2011</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
