  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/law#negligence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#LawTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">negligence</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">*Donoghue v Stevenson* [1932] AC 562 (HL)</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">failure of reasonable care</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">foreseeability</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">negligence</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">the neighbour principle</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Failing to take reasonable care against harm one could reasonably foresee</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A deployer who could foresee harm to a group of users and didn't look</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Lord Atkin's neighbour principle, House of Lords, 3–2, 26 May 1932; later structured by *Caparo v Dickman* (1990)</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">negligence</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Judges a person's conduct; the ontology borrows its foreseeability standard</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Claude Opus 5.5 (this session)</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Web 2026-11-17, secondary sources only (study guides; full judgment on BAILII not opened)</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Law</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Lord Atkin's neighbour principle in *Donoghue v Stevenson* (1932) holds that you must take reasonable care to avoid acts or omissions that you can reasonably foresee would injure your neighbour, meaning anyone so closely and directly affected that you ought to have them in mind. As a spoke, it gives the negligent kind a tested standard: the question is not whether a lens saw a harm, but whether it ought reasonably to have seen it. That makes "negligent" assessable rather than merely accusatory. The grade is relatedMatch because negligence judges a person's conduct, while the hub concerns what a lens leaves out; the ontology borrows the foreseeability test, not the tort. The fit with AI is direct. A deployer who could foresee harm to a group of users and built no way of looking for it has a negligent blind spot in this sense. The later *Caparo* test (1990) adds proximity and whether a duty would be fair, just and reasonable, which shows how much judgement even the legal version contains.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Lord Atkin</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">relatedMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">a standard for judging conduct, not a property of a lens</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#negligentBlindSpot"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">1932</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
