  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/phil#junzi">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#PhilosophyTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">junzi 君子 vs xiaoren 小人</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">*Analects* 4.16; Cicero, *De Finibus* III.16–22</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">junzi 君子 vs xiaoren 小人</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">oikeiōsis</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Degree of moral cultivation, shown in how widely concern extends</skos:definition>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">The source note records this spoke jointly with phil:oikeiosis as one combined column; kept here as one record under phil:junzi.</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">"The junzi understands what is right; the xiaoren understands what is profitable"</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Confucius moralized *junzi* (5th c. BCE); Stoic *oikeiōsis* preserved by Cicero (45 BCE)</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">junzi 君子 vs xiaoren 小人</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Cultivation of a person, extended here to lenses</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Classical Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Classical review, `developmentalAltitude` row</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Philosophy (Confucian; Stoic)</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Confucius performed a quiet revolution: *junzi* had meant someone noble by birth, and he made it mean someone noble by cultivation. The Stoics independently described moral growth as widening appropriation, from caring for one's own body to caring for humanity. Both traditions give strong ancient precedent for the author's idea that perspectives differ in the inclusivity of their value structure, independently of the size of the group they address. That distinction, accepted by the author, is one of the most useful in the structure. **For AI harm:** the same social scope can be held at very different altitudes, for example a national AI strategy framed around competitive advantage versus one framed around global welfare. **Limit, and it is serious:** every altitude scale is built from inside a tradition, and it places that tradition near the top. Confucian, Stoic, Kohlbergian and Integral scales disagree about what "higher" means; the Ethics review documents how Kohlberg's scale ranked care reasoning lower. Modelling implication: never assert altitude as a bare fact. Make it a qualified, assessed value with `assessedBy` → PerspectiveType, so every ranking states whose ranking it is, and disagreements become Divergences.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:explanationProvenance xml:lang="en">verbatim, [[v-0.01 ClassicalCouncil Review of - PerspectiveStructure 0.01|Classical review]]</ho:explanationProvenance>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Confucius; Mencius; Zeno; Chrysippus; Cicero</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">cultivation shown in how widely concern extends</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#developmentalAltitude"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">5th c. BCE / 45 BCE</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
