  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/pol#sprachregelung">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#PoliticalTheoryTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">language rule</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Arendt, *Eichmann in Jerusalem* (1963), ch. VI</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Sprachregelung</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">doublespeak</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">euphemism</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">language rule</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Official rules of language that make harmful practices sound neutral or benign</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">"Content moderation" for removal; "engagement optimization" for attention capture</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Arendt's term for Nazi euphemisms such as "final solution" and "special treatment"</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">language rule</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Deliberate concealment only; honest drift doesn't count</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Logic Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Logic review, `languageRules` row</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Political theory</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Arendt used the Nazi bureaucracy's *Sprachregelungen*, language rules such as "final solution" and "special treatment", to show how official vocabulary let people take part in atrocity without naming it. As a spoke, it is a narrower case of incongruence: deliberate, organized euphemism that conceals what is done and by whom. Honest drift between name and practice doesn't count, which is why it is graded narrowMatch. The extreme historical case must not be equated with ordinary AI vocabulary, but the mechanism is the same, and it adds something the other spokes lack: incongruence as a managed system of words, maintained by rules, not just an accidental gap. Orwell's essay on political language and the later word "doublespeak" belong to the same family. For the book, candidates include "content moderation" for removal, "engagement optimization" for attention capture and "hallucination" for fabrication. Euphemism is sometimes a legitimate softening, so the attribute should record function and context, not condemn by default.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Hannah Arendt; George Orwell; William Lutz</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">narrowMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">one kind of incongruence, deliberate euphemism</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#congruence"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">1963</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#commensurability">
