  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/sci#cargoCultScience">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#ScienceStudiesTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">cargo cult science</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Feynman, "Cargo Cult Science", *Engineering and Science* 37(7) (1974): 10–13</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">cargo cult science</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">form without substance</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">scientism</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A practice that imitates the forms of rigorous inquiry without the integrity that makes them work</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">AI benchmarks reported to decimal precision on contaminated test sets</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Feynman's 1974 Caltech commencement address</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">cargo cult science</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Scientific form and method, not stated purpose</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Physics Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Physics review, `cargoCult` row</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Philosophy of science</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Feynman's 1974 commencement address compared some research to islanders building imitation runways in the hope that planes would land: the form of science without the integrity that makes it work. As a spoke, it adds incongruence between method and substance. A lens can carry every outward sign of rigour (metrics, benchmarks, papers, decimal places) while lacking controls, replication and honest reporting of failure. It is graded relatedMatch because it concerns epistemic practice rather than a stated moral purpose, though the two often travel together when a lens's scientific form is what licenses its authority. For AI the risk is high: benchmarks and evaluations are abundant, while independent replication and reported failures are scarce. The spoke links congruence to the Epistemics group, especially Popper's falsifiers and corroboration. Because "cargo cult" is an accusation, it must be recorded as an Assessment with `assessedBy`, never as a plain fact about a lens.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Richard Feynman</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">relatedMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">about epistemic form vs substance, not a stated moral purpose</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#congruence"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">1974</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#grp_0_data">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#DatatypeProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">0 CORE (required)</rdfs:label>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">DISPLAY GROUP (PerspectiveStructure 0.02): an abstract grouping property so the property tree mirrors the diagram. Do not use it in data. Groups are display facets, not OWL classes. Data-property side of the group (OWL keeps the two trees apart).</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#PerspectiveType"/>
    <ho:displayGroup xml:lang="en">0 CORE (required)</ho:displayGroup>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#omission">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">omission</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:source xml:lang="en">PerspectiveStructure 0.02, group 11</dcterms:source>
    <dcterms:source xml:lang="en">Vocabulary Crosswalk - Concept Hubs and Discipline Schemes (HO-II.d.2), hub hov:omission</dcterms:source>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">hub drafted in HO-II.d.2; replaces blindSpot</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#PerspectiveType"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#Assessment"/>
    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#grp_11_obj"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Something bearing on a lens's verdicts that the lens leaves out, whether by design, oversight, suppression or choice.</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A benchmark that measures accuracy and leaves out dignity.</skos:example>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Cross-discipline concept hub, with three branches: out of frame, blind spot (negligent or suppressed), accepted harm</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:boundaryTests xml:lang="en">**Out of frame vs blind spot:** could this lens, used with reasonable care, have seen it? **Negligent vs suppressed:** did the lens suspect it and take deliberate steps not to find out (the *Global-Tech* test)? **Blind spot vs accepted harm:** was it seen and traded off?</ho:boundaryTests>
    <ho:designStatus xml:lang="en">proposed (kind and range assigned by the builder; to be reviewed)</ho:designStatus>
    <ho:displayGroup xml:lang="en">11 ASSESSMENTS (each an Assessment with assessedBy -&gt; PerspectiveType)</ho:displayGroup>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Every lens leaves things out; the question is how and why. The council reviews found four kinds of exclusion that the 0.01 structure lumped together as `blindSpot`. Taking a blind spot in its ordinary sense, something visible but missed, sorts them into three branches. **Out of frame:** excluded by design and not visible to this lens at all, as a map must leave things out (constitutive). **Blind spot:** visible, but missed, either through lack of reasonable care (negligent) or because the lens suspected it and arranged not to look (suppressed). **Accepted harm:** seen and tolerated as the price of a benefit. Each calls for a different response: out-of-frame omissions need another lens to cover them; negligent blind spots need better instruments or care; suppressed ones need transparency, disclosure and whistleblower protection; accepted harms need moral and political argument. Keeping them apart stops the worse kinds hiding behind the better ones, for example "out of scope" used as an alibi for suppression. Which branch applies is a judgement, so it is always an Assessment with `assessedBy`. Links: [[HO-005 Assessed Membership and Systematic Omission|HO-005]], [[HO-004 Criticality, Leverage and the Limits of Scope|HO-004]].</ho:explanation>
    <ho:glossLine xml:lang="en">What a lens leaves out of its verdicts.</ho:glossLine>
    <ho:hubStatus xml:lang="en">candidate</ho:hubStatus>
    <ho:tier xml:lang="en">★</ho:tier>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#CausalGraph">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">CausalGraph</rdfs:label>
