  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/law#congruence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#LawTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">congruence</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Fuller, *The Morality of Law* (Yale UP, 1964; rev. ed. 1969)</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">congruence</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">congruence between official action and declared rule</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">the eighth desideratum</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The requirement that officials actually apply the rules that have been declared</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A content policy enforced against one community but not another</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Set out through the parable of King Rex; followed the Hart–Fuller debate (1958)</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">congruence</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Rule systems and their officials, not persons' intentions</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Legal Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Legal review, `proceduralIntegrity` row</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Law</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Fuller's eighth desideratum asks whether the rules officials actually apply are the rules that were declared. As a spoke of the congruence hub, it contributes the most precise and testable version of the idea: compare the published rule with observed official action. That makes congruence something you can measure from enforcement records, not only argue about, and it connects directly to Holmes's `enforcementReality` and to Pound's distinction between law in books and law in action. It differs from the hub in scope. Fuller audits rule systems and the officials who run them. He doesn't ask about one agent's private maxim, as Kant does, or about deliberate euphemism, as Arendt does. For AI harm the test applies almost unchanged: a platform's published content policy against its moderation logs, or a model card's stated purpose against how the model is actually deployed. Hart's caution carries over too. A perfectly congruent system can still be unjust, so congruence is necessary for integrity but not sufficient for goodness.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Lon Fuller; H. L. A. Hart (critic); Joseph Raz</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">the same idea, but restricted to rule systems and their officials</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#congruence"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">1964</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#coupledWith">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">coupledWith</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:source xml:lang="en">PerspectiveStructure 0.02, group 1</dcterms:source>
