    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#grp_1_obj"/>
    <ho:designStatus xml:lang="en">proposed (kind and range assigned by the builder; to be reviewed)</ho:designStatus>
    <ho:displayGroup xml:lang="en">1 RELATIONS (relations before attributes)</ho:displayGroup>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#Person">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Person</rdfs:label>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">stub</skos:definition>
    <ho:designStatus xml:lang="en">stub: range target only in this experiment</ho:designStatus>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/eth#operativeMaxim">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#EthicsTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">operative maxim</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Kant, *Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals* (1785), Ak. 4:421</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">hidden maxim</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">maxim vs stated end</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">operative maxim</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The subjective principle an agent actually acts on, which may differ from the end they profess</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A lens stated as "safety" whose maxim is "observe everyone, in case"</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">The categorical imperative tests maxims, not stated goals</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">operative maxim</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">One agent's principle of action, not institutional practice</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Ethics Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Ethics review, `normativeForm: deontic` row (Ak. 4:421)</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Ethics</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">For Kant, the moral test applies to the maxim, the principle an agent actually acts on, not to the end the agent professes. Someone can sincerely state a good end while acting on a maxim that couldn't be willed as universal law. As a spoke, it adds the idea that incongruence can be hidden from the agent itself, not only from others. Kant's concept concerns one agent's will, while the hub concerns a lens's public identity and institutional operation, but the two meet closely enough to grade it closeMatch. They meet when a lens is treated as the maxim of an institution: a lens stated as "safety" whose working principle is "observe everyone, in case". That reading is an interpretation for this ontology, not Kant's own application. It explains why stated purpose and operative purpose should be recorded separately, with the operative side as an Assessment. For AI it matters because a deployer's stated purpose is usually sincere and still uninformative about what the system is optimized to do.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Immanuel Kant; Christine Korsgaard</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">(author, 2026-11-17): a lens's operative purpose is read as its maxim; Kant's own term concerns an individual agent's principle</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#congruence"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">1785</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#congruence">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">congruence</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:source xml:lang="en">PerspectiveStructure 0.02, group 11</dcterms:source>
    <dcterms:source xml:lang="en">Vocabulary Crosswalk - Concept Hubs and Discipline Schemes (HO-II.d.2), hub hov:congruence</dcterms:source>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">stated vs operative; hub drafted in HO-II.d.2</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#PerspectiveType"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#Assessment"/>
    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#grp_11_obj"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The degree to which what a lens says it is and does (its name, stated purpose, declared rules) matches what it actually does in practice.</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A "trust &amp; safety" function whose real remit is reputational protection.</skos:example>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Cross-discipline concept hub</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:designStatus xml:lang="en">proposed (kind and range assigned by the builder; to be reviewed)</ho:designStatus>
    <ho:displayGroup xml:lang="en">11 ASSESSMENTS (each an Assessment with assessedBy -&gt; PerspectiveType)</ho:displayGroup>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Five disciplines, working from very different starting points, name the same gap between a lens's declared identity and its actual operation. Law calls it congruence between declared rule and official action; Confucian philosophy, the rectification of names; Kantian ethics, the difference between a professed end and the maxim actually acted on; political theory, the language rules that hide what is done; philosophy of science, cargo cult science, the form of rigour without its substance. The hub gives the idea a plain name so that no discipline's term is privileged, and each spoke keeps its own vocabulary and says where it differs. For the book this is central: many AI harms are carried by names ("safety", "alignment", "personalization") that a practice can claim without earning. Because judging incongruence is an evaluation, the ontology records it as an Assessment with `assessedBy`, never as a plain fact about the lens. The five-way convergence is the strongest in the council reviews, but all five were voiced by one model, so it counts as a candidate, not as independent confirmation.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:glossLine xml:lang="en">The fit between what a lens says it does and what it actually does.</ho:glossLine>
    <ho:hubStatus xml:lang="en">candidate</ho:hubStatus>
    <ho:tier xml:lang="en">★★</ho:tier>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/phil#zhengming">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#PhilosophyTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">rectification of names</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">*Analects* 13.3; *Xunzi* ch. 22</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">correcting names</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">rectification of names</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">zhengming 正名</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The demand that names match the realities and duties they claim</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A "trust &amp; safety" team whose real remit is reputational risk</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Confucius's answer on what he would do first in government; Xunzi made names conventions enforced by rulers</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">rectification of names</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Names and titles, not operations directly</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Classical Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Classical review, `name` row</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Philosophy (Confucian)</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">In *Analects* 13.3 Confucius says the first act of government would be to rectify names, because when names are wrong, speech and affairs fail. As a spoke, it locates incongruence in naming: a name is a promise about function, and a name that claims a virtue the practice lacks corrupts everything done under it. It maps closely to the hub but starts from the other end. Law checks action against a declared rule; *zhengming* checks a name against the reality and duties it claims. Xunzi sharpened it into a political point: names are conventions enforced by those with power, so deciding which name is "correct" is itself a perspective. That is why the ontology should record naming disputes as Divergences, not silently pick a winner. For AI harm the pattern is common: "safety" that means brand protection, "personalization" that means surveillance, "responsible AI" that means a principles page. The spoke supplies the vocabulary of `nameIntegrity`: apt, euphemistic, inflated, captured.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Confucius; Xunzi</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">the same gap, located in names and titles</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#congruence"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">c. 5th c. BCE</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
