  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/int#altitude">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#IntegralTheoryTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">altitude</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">**Primary:** Wilber, "The Leading Edge of the Unknown in the Human Being" (talk transcript, Integral Life, undated) for bands, Gebser stages and tiers; Wilber, "Integral Semiotics" (excerpt from unpublished vol. 2 of the Kosmos Trilogy, undated) for colours red/amber/orange/green/indigo and quadrants. **Secondary:** Wilber, *The Religion of Tomorrow* (2017), p. 349, for the 12-colour sequence, as quoted by Visser. AQAL origin: *Sex, Ecology, Spirituality* (1995).</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">altitude</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">colour altitudes</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">egocentric, ethnocentric, worldcentric, kosmocentric</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The level of development, marked by how widely a person or perspective identifies and cares</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A tribe-scale group holding worldcentric values</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Wilber says his four bands are Gilligan's four stages, "a condensed version of the more standard 6-to-8 levels", and takes stage names from Gebser. The colour scheme and level numbering change between texts (e.g. red is "Level 10" in "Integral Semiotics"). His colours resemble Spiral Dynamics but aren't identical.</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">altitude</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Applies to whole perspectives as well as persons; the most comprehensive scale, and the most contested</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Claude Opus 5.5 (this session)</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Primary texts read in full 2026-11-17: [Leading Edge PDF](http://integral-life-home.s3.amazonaws.com/Wilber-WakeUpGrowUp-TheEdgeOfTheUnknown.pdf); [Integral Semiotics PDF](https://integral-life-home.s3.amazonaws.com/Ken%20Wilber%20-%20Integral%20Semiotics.pdf). 12-colour sequence: secondary only ([Visser](https://www.integralworld.net/visser101.html)); book not seen. SES 1995 as AQAL origin: [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sex,_Ecology,_Spirituality).</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Integral theory</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Wilber's Integral theory is where the author's term comes from. In it, "altitude" means level of development, and the moral sequence runs egocentric (care for oneself), ethnocentric (care for one's own group), worldcentric (care for all people, regardless of race, sex or creed) and kosmocentric (care for all sentient beings). Wilber presents the scale as a measure of how widely a person identifies. In his own text, the four bands are Gilligan's four stages, and they group Gebser's finer stages: "the archaic and magic stages are both egocentric; the mythic stage … is ethnocentric; the rational and pluralistic stages are worldcentric; and the integral stage is integrated or kosmocentric." As a spoke, it contributes the most comprehensive scale and the clearest statement of the size-versus-altitude distinction. A tribe can be worldcentric; an empire can be ethnocentric. Its colour altitudes give a still finer vocabulary: 12 colours from infrared to white in *The Religion of Tomorrow* (2017), as quoted by Visser, though the scheme and its numbering change between Wilber's texts and overlap confusingly with Spiral Dynamics. Two cautions travel with it. Its correlations with other scales are Wilber's claims, and critics such as Frank Visser contest some of them. And as a scale built from inside one tradition, it is subject to the same "whose ranking" rule as Kohlberg's. Its AQAL quadrants also supply the other two integral properties, interior/exterior and individual/collective.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:explanationProvenance xml:lang="en">new draft</ho:explanationProvenance>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Ken Wilber; Jean Gebser (stage names); Carol Gilligan (bands); Clare Graves and Spiral Dynamics (colour precursor); Frank Visser (critic)</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">the sequence is defined by widening identity and care; the source of the author's term</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#developmentalAltitude"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">per text (undated talk; 2017 for colours)</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#hubStatus">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#AnnotationProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">hub status</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#developmentalAltitude">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">developmentalAltitude</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:source xml:lang="en">PerspectiveStructure 0.02, group 3.2, 11</dcterms:source>
    <dcterms:source xml:lang="en">Vocabulary Crosswalk - Concept Hubs and Discipline Schemes (HO-II.d.2), hub hov:developmentalAltitude</dcterms:source>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">an Assessment; scale + assessedBy required; displayed in group 3, belongs to group 11</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#PerspectiveType"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#Assessment"/>
    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#grp_11_obj"/>
    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#grp_3_2_obj"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">How widely and how reflectively a lens's value structure extends concern, independently of the size of the group it addresses.</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">Two national AI strategies with the same social scope, one framed around competitive advantage, the other around global welfare.</skos:example>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Cross-discipline concept hub; one of three scope axes with `socialScope` (size) and `moralCircle` (which beings count)</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:designStatus xml:lang="en">proposed (kind and range assigned by the builder; to be reviewed)</ho:designStatus>
    <ho:displayGroup xml:lang="en">11 ASSESSMENTS (each an Assessment with assessedBy -&gt; PerspectiveType)</ho:displayGroup>
    <ho:displayGroup xml:lang="en">3.2 scope axes (kept separate)</ho:displayGroup>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Several traditions, working independently, describe the same thing: values that widen from self, to one's own group, to all people, and sometimes beyond. Confucius made *junzi* a rank of cultivation rather than birth; the Stoics described concern widening outward from oneself; Kohlberg measured stages of moral reasoning, and Gilligan answered with stages of care; Wilber's Integral theory takes Gilligan's four stages as the bands egocentric, ethnocentric, worldcentric and kosmocentric. The author's distinction holds across all of them: a tribe-sized lens can hold world-centric values, so altitude is not size. The attribute is powerful because a lens organized around self-interest really does differ from one organized around universal concern, and the difference shapes which harms it counts. It is dangerous because every scale is built from inside a tradition and tends to put that tradition near the top. Gilligan's critique of Kohlberg is the documented case. So the ontology never stores altitude as a bare value: each value belongs to a named scale, is an Assessment with `assessedBy`, and disagreements between scales are recorded as Divergences. For the book, it matters when AI developers claim their systems align to "universal" or "higher" values: the question is always whose scale.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:glossLine xml:lang="en">How widely and reflectively a lens's values extend concern.</ho:glossLine>
    <ho:hubStatus xml:lang="en">candidate</ho:hubStatus>
  </rdf:Description>
