  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/sci#agnotology">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#ScienceStudiesTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">agnotology</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Proctor &amp; Schiebinger (eds.), *Agnotology* (Stanford UP, 2008); McGoey, *The Unknowers* (Zed, 2019)</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">agnotology</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">manufactured doubt</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">strategic ignorance</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The study of ignorance deliberately produced or maintained</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">Avoiding data collection on harms that would create obligations</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">"Agnotology" coined 1992; tobacco industry's manufactured doubt as the founding case</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">agnotology</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Institutional and strategic</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Cognitive Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Cognitive review, `suppressors` row</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">History of science</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Agnotology is the study of ignorance that is produced or maintained, not simply not-yet-known. Proctor developed it from the tobacco industry's efforts to manufacture doubt about the harms of smoking; McGoey extended it to "strategic ignorance" in organizations. As a spoke, it gives the suppressed kind its institutional, evidence-based form. Where Minsky describes the mechanism inside one mind, agnotology documents how institutions arrange not to know: funding counter-research, avoiding studies whose results would create obligations, and designing record-keeping so that certain facts never exist on paper. It is graded closeMatch because it describes the same thing as the kind, at the scale the ontology cares about. For AI harm the pattern is plausible and worth watching: companies that avoid measuring harms that would trigger duties, or systems built so certain data are never logged. Any claim about a specific organization needs its own sourced research. The link to HO-II.e matters here: a value that is Undisclosed, held by the party with most to lose, can be evidence of suppression.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Robert Proctor; Londa Schiebinger; Linsey McGoey</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">produced ignorance at institutional scale</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#suppressedBlindSpot"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">2008</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
