  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/phil#wu">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#PhilosophyTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">wu 無 (non-being)</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">*Daodejing* ch. 11</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">the empty vessel</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">usefulness of emptiness</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">wu 無 (non-being)</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An exclusion that makes the lens usable</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">A map that leaves out most of the territory so it can be read</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Text dated c. 4th c. BCE; Guodian slips (excavated 1993)</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">wu 無 (non-being)</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Applies to the lens's design, not its users</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Classical Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Classical review, `blindSpot (constitutive)` row (partly verified: Lau edition not confirmed)</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Philosophy (Daoist)</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">In chapter 11 of the *Daodejing*, the usefulness of a cart, a pot and a room comes from their empty space: thirty spokes share one hub, and it is the emptiness at the centre that makes the wheel turn. As a spoke, this gives the constitutive kind its clearest image. A lens is useful because of what it leaves out. A map that showed everything would be as large as the territory and useless. This reverses the 0.01 structure's assumption that a blind spot is a defect. Some exclusions are the price of a lens working at all, and the right response to them is not to repair the lens but to make sure another lens covers what it leaves out. The danger is that the image can be borrowed as an excuse. "Out of scope" sounds constitutive even when the exclusion serves the lens-holder's interests. That is why the kind is assessed, not declared: a lens's own claim that an exclusion is constitutive is evidence, not a verdict. For AI harm, a benchmark that measures accuracy legitimately ignores dignity only if some other lens is watching dignity.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Laozi (traditional); Wang Bi</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">closeMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">usefulness through emptiness is the constitutive kind</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#outOfFrame"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">c. 4th c. BCE</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
