  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/sci#anomaly">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#ScienceStudiesTerm"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">anomaly</rdfs:label>
    <dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Kuhn, *The Structure of Scientific Revolutions* (1962), ch. VI</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">anomaly</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">outlier</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">residual</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">An observation a lens can't explain and tends to set aside</skos:definition>
    <skos:example xml:lang="en">Early complaints about an algorithm that fit no recognized harm category</skos:example>
    <skos:historyNote xml:lang="en">Kuhn argued accumulating anomalies precede scientific revolutions</skos:historyNote>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">anomaly</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Names what is set aside, not why</skos:scopeNote>
    <ho:assertedBy xml:lang="en">Physics Council</ho:assertedBy>
    <ho:citationVerification xml:lang="en">Physics review, `anomalies` row</ho:citationVerification>
    <ho:discipline xml:lang="en">Philosophy of science</ho:discipline>
    <ho:explanation xml:lang="en">Kuhn argued that normal science sets aside observations its paradigm can't explain, and that these anomalies accumulate until they force a revolution. Curie's pitchblende, more radioactive than its uranium could explain, is the classic case of an anomaly taken seriously. As a spoke, it maps to the negligent kind with relatedMatch, because setting an anomaly aside isn't always negligent: sometimes it is reasonable, and the anomaly is noise. What it adds is a way to see negligence as it forms. Anomalies are the visible residue of a blind spot, the cases the lens couldn't place. For AI harm, they are often individual complaints that fit no recognized harm category, unusual model behaviour, or patterns among users that no lens has a concept for. These are frequently the first evidence of a new kind of harm. Recording anomalies, rather than letting them drop, is how a lens's negligent blind spots become visible before the damage does. It links this hub to `epistemicStanding` in the Assessments group.</ho:explanation>
    <ho:keyThinkers xml:lang="en">Thomas Kuhn; Marie Curie</ho:keyThinkers>
    <ho:mappingGrade xml:lang="en">relatedMatch</ho:mappingGrade>
    <ho:mappingReason xml:lang="en">what a paradigm sets aside, which may be negligent or merely premature</ho:mappingReason>
    <ho:mapsToProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/harm-ontology/v3-editor-mini/schema#negligentBlindSpot"/>
    <ho:meaningAsOf xml:lang="en">1962</ho:meaningAsOf>
  </rdf:Description>
